I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and the provided PXRD/GSAS-II tooling. I’ll keep a candidate ledger and establish a falsifiable phase inventory before refining cells, profiles, or structures.

The chemistry points first to CuWO₄, with residual CuO/WO₃ and Cu–W–O intermediates as mandatory alternatives. I’m now extracting peak families and checking the frozen COD inventory so that polymorphs and analogue-derived prototypes are compared on the same footing.

Initial peak mapping already separates at least three families: a shifted/broadened triclinic CuWO₄ family, tenorite CuO, and a sharp cuprite Cu₂O family. Several low-angle and 33–35° peaks remain unassigned, so I’m treating those as an open inventory branch rather than forcing them into those three phases.

I’m formalizing the inventory now. The main-phase shortlist will include both reported CuWO₄ polymorphs and target-composition models made from one-substitution wolframite/triclinic analogues; CuO, Cu₂O, Cu₄O₃, all available WO₃ polymorphs, Cu₃WO₆, and Cu₂WO₄ remain separate branches. Each constructed model will be composition-, occupancy-, symmetry-, and contact-screened before any fit.

The first stable branch converged cleanly (single-phase triclinic CuWO₄, Rwp 32.37%), but its effective zero shift is large and many coherent residuals remain, as expected for an incomplete inventory. I’m now replaying the full CuWO₄ topology shortlist with identical background, shift, cell, and fixed-profile settings; failed or invalid branches will remain “unknown,” not be counted as rejections.

The common-setting topology comparison is still running. The important diagnostic will be whether a candidate improves the low-angle splitting without requiring a distorted cell; Rwp alone will not decide the structure.

The principal topology result is decisive: published triclinic P-1 CuWO₄ fits far better than every P2/c or analogue-derived alternative under the common model (Rwp 32.37% versus 51.88–55.03%). I’m retaining the diverse shortlist in the ledger but moving P-1 forward while the one-at-a-time secondary-phase closure trials finish.

The secondary screen is slower for the low-symmetry and large-cell candidates, but it is progressing without intervention. I’m preserving each pre-stage project so a numerical failure cannot be misclassified as a scientific rejection.

Using the stable project corrected an important screening artifact: CuO now contributes positively at about 3.7 wt% (Rwp 32.37→32.24), while monoclinic WO₃ gives a much larger, stable improvement to Rwp 19.97 at about 23.9 wt%. The remaining branches are being rerun from that same stable state before any phase is accepted or rejected.

The large-cell Cu₂WO₄/WO₃ trials are still the slow cases. No branch will be promoted merely for absorbing intensity: I’ll also require a sensible cell, positive scale, and a residual family that disappears where that phase predicts it.

The residual families now close chemically and metrically: the peaks at 15.18, 19.80, 32.51, and 33.79° track cubic Cu₃WO₆ after the common angular shift, while the 22.7–24.0° triplet and its higher-angle harmonics track monoclinic WO₃. Cu₂O’s trial was numerically invalid and remains unknown; it is not being used as evidence against cuprite. I’m now testing the CuWO₄ + WO₃ + Cu₃WO₆ inventory from both addition orders.

The three-phase inventory is reproducible from both addition orders: Rwp 10.336%, with approximately 65.36 wt% CuWO₄, 19.53 wt% WO₃, and 15.12 wt% Cu₃WO₆ before profile refinement. I’m testing whether the remaining residual shoulders support a fourth phase or are explained by peak-width/position mismatch.

The repaired Cu₂WO₄ branch is deliberately being allowed to complete because its original CIF could not be imported; the repair only reserialized the published P1 cell and 28 sites. Until it finishes, that branch remains unknown rather than rejected.

The fourth-phase test supports tenorite CuO: it gives the largest stable improvement (Rwp 10.34→9.77) at ~3.0 wt%, with a near-reference C2/c cell and a coherent peak family. Cu₄O₃, Cu₂O, Cu₂WO₄, a second CuWO₄ polymorph, and a second WO₃ polymorph contribute only 0.15–2.18 wt% and much smaller gains, so they remain provisional until the simpler four-phase profile is optimized. A simultaneous per-phase size refinement hit bounds and produced negative microstrain, so I have rolled it back.

The simpler broadening model is now stable: refining one isotropic CuWO₄ microstrain term (≈2197 ppm) lowers Rwp to 6.99 without a boundary hit; adding crystallite size gives negligible extra benefit and is being omitted. I’m next testing symmetry-allowed heavy-atom coordinates one phase at a time, with all nuisance parameters fixed, because the remaining intensity residuals are not evidence for another phase by themselves.

Heavy-atom refinements are stable and physically economical: CuWO₄, WO₃, and Cu₃WO₆ heavy-site coordinates lower Rwp from 6.98 to 6.05 in sequence; CuO has no symmetry-free Cu coordinate and does not improve. Oxygen-coordinate trials are running separately so that any gain can be screened against contact distances and parameter count.

The WO₃ oxygen refinement is rejected despite lowering Rwp: it drives W–O to 1.13 Å and O–O to 1.78 Å, so the profile gain is not physically admissible. I’m retaining the heavy-atom-only WO₃ model and screening the other oxygen branches before deciding whether any are supportable.

Common phase-level Uiso terms are strongly supported for CuWO₄, WO₃, and Cu₃WO₆ and refine to positive values (about 0.011, 0.021, and 0.0096 Å²). The CuO Uiso trial crossed the zero bound and produced a negative ADP, so that branch is rejected and CuO retains its published 0.01 Å² value.

The physically admissible four-phase model now reaches Rwp 4.54%, with common positive Uiso terms for the three W-bearing phases. I’m retesting every previously marginal fifth phase against this refined baseline; this is the closure check that distinguishes a genuine minor phase from earlier peak-shape compensation.

A full common-profile re-refinement lowers Rwp to 4.14%, but U hits its zero bound. I’m therefore comparing reduced profile models with U or V held fixed; I’ll retain the simplest model that keeps the analytic peak width positive and avoids an actively boundary-hitting parameter.

The remaining residuals are dominated by peak-shape derivatives rather than new peak families. I’m testing one additional complexity only: uniaxial CuWO₄ microstrain along the three crystallographic axes, each from the same stable four-phase model. It will be retained only if the axial/equatorial values stay positive and the improvement is axis-specific and stable.

March–Dollase screening gives ratios within 0.4% of random orientation for every CuWO₄ axis and only marginal gains, so no texture term is retained. Before finalizing, I’m revisiting the fixed crystallite-size/strain values of the three minor phases one at a time under the now-stable instrument profile; earlier simultaneous refinement was invalid, but these identity-specific tests can distinguish genuine broadening from a common-profile artifact.

The minor-phase broadening tests clarify the earlier instability. WO₃ stays near the existing isotropic values. CuO and Cu₃WO₆ drive crystallite-size broadening to zero (the 10 µm numerical ceiling), while Cu₃WO₆ also supports a lower isotropic strain if size is held at 0.5 µm. I’m testing the simpler interpretation—instrument-limited CuO/Cu₃WO₆ peaks with their size terms fixed inactive—rather than reporting boundary values as measured crystallite sizes.

The phase inventory is now closed at four crystalline phases; I’m finishing the last constrained line-broadening pass, then I’ll replay the project and cross-check the exported CIFs, fractions, and profile against it. The current best fit is already near Rwp 3.30%, with no coherent unassigned peak family.

The three minor-phase broadening terms are now interior and stable: isotropic microstrain for WO₃, Cu₃WO₆, and CuO, while their crystallite-size terms remain fixed and are not interpreted. I’m doing a coordinate-descent replay of the shared instrument profile, cells/zero, and structural parameters so the final project is converged as a whole rather than only stage-by-stage.

The shared-cell/zero and common-Uiso passes remained chemically sensible and converged. I’m now replaying only the coordinate subsets that were supported in the earlier screen—heavy atoms first, oxygen only where its geometry stayed physical—and will reject any branch that recreates short W–O or O–O contacts.

All retained coordinate refinements pass the physical screen: full occupancies, expected space groups, minimum cation–oxygen distances of 1.71–1.93 Å, and minimum O–O distances of 2.45–2.61 Å. The unconstrained oxygen branches previously rejected for WO₃ and Cu₃WO₆ remain excluded. I’m now doing one final nuisance-parameter loop and will then freeze and replay the exact submitted project.

The attempted post-cell full profile replay became numerically stuck (V/W correlation) and was rolled back, so it is marked unknown rather than accepted or rejected. I’m testing the supported reduced W/X/axial-asymmetry subset from the stable checkpoint; if that also fails, the prior stable profile remains the final model.

The final stable checkpoint is converged (maximum shift/esd 0.00034) at Rwp 3.193%. The residual extrema occur as adjacent negative/positive pairs at the strongest reflections, consistent with small peak-shape/position mismatch rather than a new coherent peak family. I’ve rolled back both late profile retries because they aborted; I’m now materializing and independently replaying the stable project.

Independent validation is clean: the project replayed from atomic structure factors with zero parameters re-optimized, reproduced `fit.csv` to better than 10⁻¹¹ counts, and raised no negative-width/background/displacement flags. All four CIFs also pass the submission parser’s composition, occupancy, and contact screens, and the fractions sum exactly to one.

Completed the four-phase GSAS-II refinement.

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| CuWO₄, P-1 | 63.79% |
| WO₃, P2₁/c | 20.00% |
| Cu₃WO₆, Pa-3 | 13.76% |
| CuO, C2/c | 2.46% |

Final fit: Rwp 3.1926%, Rp 2.4261%, GOF 1.9772%. Native GSAS-II replay reproduced the profile exactly and raised no physical-profile flags.

Fractions are crystalline-phase normalized; amorphous content is not quantified. CuO has the greatest relative uncertainty, and trace Cu₂O cannot be conclusively excluded.

